\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The anti-Fibonacci numbers are $A_n=\tfrac{n(n+1)}{2}+1$ $(n\ge 0)$, that is, $1,2,4,7,11,16,\dots$, defined by $A_0=1$ and $A_n=A_{n-1}+n$.
We study the set $\R$ of integers of the form $A_a+A_b$ and its counting function $R(N)$.
We prove that $m\in\R$ if and only if $8m-14$ is a sum of two integer squares, equivalently if and only if $m\ge2$ and $4m-7$ is a sum of two squares.
This gives a Fermat-type criterion in terms of the prime factorisation of $4m-7$.
From it we derive the following.
(i)~A fixed local obstruction at $3$: no $m\equiv 1,7\pmod 9$ is representable, so at least $2k$ of the integers in $[0,9k)$ are not representable.
(ii)~An exact Chinese-remainder count: for a finite set $S$ of primes $p\equiv3\pmod4$, exactly $\prod_{p\in S}(p^2-p+1)$ residues modulo $\prod_{p\in S}p^2$ satisfy all local conditions.
(iii)~The explicit sieve bound $\#\{m<N: m\in\R\}\le (N/Q+1)\prod_{p\in S}(p^2-p+1)$.
(iv)~The Euler-product inequality $\prod_{p\in S}(1-(p-1)/p^2)\le(1+\tfrac12\sum_{p\in S}1/p)^{-1}$.
We also prove that $\sum_{p\equiv a\ (q)}1/p=\infty$ for every unit $a$ modulo $q$, by a tail argument from the standard lower bound for the Dirichlet series of the von Mangoldt function restricted to a residue class.
Together these give our main theorem: $\R$ has natural density zero.
Hence the conjectured positive density is false in the naive sense.
We then show that the reduction transfers Landau's theorem verbatim, giving $R(N)\sim 2b\,N/\sqrt{\log N}$ with $b$ the Landau--Ramanujan constant.
We give numerical evidence, explain why the elementary sieve cannot reach the $1/\sqrt{\log N}$ scale on its own, and list open problems.
\end{abstract}

