\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Representability test.}
If $m<2$, reject. Otherwise factor $n=4m-7$, and accept if and only if every prime $q\equiv3\pmod4$ divides $n$ to an even power.
Given a representation $4m-7=u^2+v^2$ with $u>v\ge0$, the indices $a=(u+v-1)/2$ and $b=(u-v-1)/2$ give $m=A_a+A_b$.

\paragraph{Counting $R(N)$.}
Enumerate the roughly $\sqrt{2N}$ anti-Fibonacci numbers $\le N$ and mark all pairwise sums in a bit array. This takes $O(N)$ time.

\paragraph{Exact CRT sieve.}
Compute $Q_S$ and $C_S$ in $O(|S|)$ operations. The admissible residues form a CRT product of local sets of sizes $p^2-p+1$.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The reduction is the structural core. It turns an additive question about a quadratic sequence into a multiplicative one.
Theorems~\ref{thm:crt} and~\ref{thm:euler} give the fully explicit inequality $R^{<}(N)/N\le(1+\frac12\sum_{p\in S}1/p)^{-1}+C_S/N$ for every admissible $S$.
The only analytic input to density zero is divergence of $\sum1/p$ over one residue class.
Up to the shift by $2$, the problem is the question of which integers are sums of two triangular numbers: $m\in\R\iff m-2=T_a+T_b$.

Open problems:
(1)~a self-contained Selberg--Delange proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:landau-af} with an explicit error term;
(2)~an effective bound $R(N)\le C\,N/\sqrt{\log N}$ via Selberg's sieve and Mertens' product in progressions;
